{
  "rules": [
    "Strong upward-trend underestimation: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rally, the model systematically forecasts too low across the full horizon (often by ~5–15 €/t, growing with lead time). Apply a positive level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/acceleration lag: the forecast path rises too slowly versus reality (missing convexity). If recent returns are positive and increasing, add an acceleration term (e.g., increasing uplift from day 1 to day 30).",
    "Excess mean-reversion bias: forecasts repeatedly drift back toward ~69–72 even when the market breaks out to mid/high-70s and 80s. In breakout regimes, reduce mean-reversion strength and anchor more to recent highs.",
    "Horizon-specific bias: short horizon (days 1–5) errors are smaller; medium/long horizon (days 10–30) errors widen materially. Use a horizon-dependent correction (uplift ramp) rather than a flat adjustment.",
    "Regime rule: if price is above the 30-day moving average and the MA slope is positive, treat as ‘bull regime’ and apply the uplift/acceleration; otherwise keep baseline (or mild mean reversion)."
  ]
}
